<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Devices this app declares an interest in, so Android can grant it permission permanently.

    The point is not auto-launching, which is a side effect we tolerate: it is the "use by default
    for this USB device" checkbox. Android only offers that when a matching intent-filter exists,
    and without one permission is asked for again on every enumeration - measured here as a dialog
    on each of six replugs, and nearly three seconds from attach to a claimed device rather than
    one.

    That matters because unplugging and replugging the pad is a real part of using it: it is the
    only way to clear the audio state a killed process leaves behind, every software route having
    been tried and failed (see jni/xow_driver/AUDIO.md). A recovery the user performs by hand should
    not also cost them a dialog.

    Cabled pads are matched on the GIP signature rather than on product IDs: vendor 0x045e, and the
    vendor-specific interface 0xff / 0x47 / 0xd0 that MS-GIPUSB 2.2.12 defines and that
    XboxOneController.canClaimDevice() already tests for. Listing product IDs instead would need
    this file edited for every pad the driver gains, and the two lists would drift.

    The wireless adapter is matched by product ID, because that is how
    XboxWirelessDongle.canClaimDevice() matches it - it takes exactly two, and its interface is one
    vendor-specific descriptor with bulk endpoints rather than the GIP layout above. Keeping each
    entry in step with the predicate that claims it is what stops this file drifting from the code.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Cabled Xbox pads: the GIP data interface -->
    <usb-device vendor-id="1118" class="255" subclass="71" protocol="208" />

    <!-- Xbox Wireless Adapter, older (0x02e6) and newer (0x02fe) -->
    <usb-device vendor-id="1118" product-id="742" />
    <usb-device vendor-id="1118" product-id="766" />
</resources>
